 2. 🫏 Dems already chasing 2028 cash 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 A vendor sold Newsom 2028 merch as the California governor campaigned for the midterms in Hardeeville, S.C., in September. Photo: Sam Wolfe/Reuters The race for money among the top Democratic 2028 contenders is underway before any of them have even said they're running.

 Why it matters: "With the first candidates expected to announce their campaigns as soon as the end of this year, there is expected to be intense financial pressure in a crowded field," The New York Times' Teddy Schleifer reports . A competitive primary campaign could cost close to $100 million. 👀 Democratic megadonors are sizing up the big players — and some are revealing early favorites, Schleifer reports: 

 Reed Hastings, Netflix co-founder, has signaled he's leaning toward California Gov. Gavin Newsom. Chris Larsen, a crypto billionaire, also likes Newsom, though he also gave $1 million to Pennsylvania Gov. Josh Shapiro's reelection campaign. Michael Bloomberg likes Shapiro and gave $2.5 million to his reelection campaign. Deborah Simon, the shopping mall heiress, is impressed by Kentucky Gov. Andy Beshear. The Soros family has hosted two events for Sen. Jon Ossoff (D-Ga.). Alex Soros has also praised Newsom and Illinois Gov. JB Pritzker, who's a billionaire himself. Elizabeth Frawley Bagley, a longtime Democratic fundraiser, has separately hosted Ossoff and Maryland Gov. Wes Moore on Nantucket. Reid Hoffman, LinkedIn co-founder, said he's spoken with several 2028 hopefuls about AI. He gave $1 million to a super PAC backing Ossoff this summer. Keep reading (gift link). 

 
 
