 3. 🪖 Exclusive: Fired general admitted leaking to reporters 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Gen. Brett Sylvia at the Defender-Europe 20 joint military exercise in Poland in 2020. Photo: Janek Skarzynski/AFP via Getty Images The Pentagon's top military planner admitted to leaking classified information about Ukraine and Iran to two reporters from separate outlets over a one-year period, according to an internal investigation report shared with Axios' Marc Caputo and Thomas Novelly. 

 Why it matters: Army Lt. Gen. Brett Sylvia was fired this week by Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth for the alleged misconduct, which Sylvia acknowledged on video after he failed four polygraph tests, a U.S. official said.

 The Pentagon said it referred a leak case to the Justice Department, and The Washington Post reports Sylvia is the accused. 🔎 Zoom in: The report and U.S. officials said that Sylvia's leaking began as early as June 2025 — before mainstream media outlets were removed from the Pentagon — and continued afterward via the secure Signal app and once at a Silver Diner in Arlington, Va.

 For another story, the report said, Sylvia "admitted to being one of the 20 officials" who criticized Pentagon leadership. Read on . 

 
 
