 5. ⚡ Cyber&#x27;s &quot;Black October&quot; 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Cybersecurity teams are being pummeled by a relentless barrage of attacks, vulnerabilities and false alarms during what cyber pros are calling " Black October ."

 Why it matters: Humans are still on the front lines of a cyber fight enabled largely by AI. They're struggling to wade through mountains of threat data to determine who's been hit and which attacks pose a real danger to companies, Axios' Sam Sabin reports. 🛡️ The big picture: The people companies call when they're hacked are being swamped, piling onto the persistent burnout that has plagued the industry.

 "The last month has been the longest year of my life," says Justin Timothy, threat intelligence consultant at GuidePoint Security. Troy Bettencourt, who leads Palo Alto Networks' global digital forensics and incident response practice, told Axios that demand for his team's services has climbed by double digits over the last six weeks. What's next: AI's ability to uncover more software flaws threatens to bury defenders in even more work.

 To keep up, security firms are turning to AI themselves to spot weaknesses and speed up investigations. Keep reading . 

 
 
